        <body>
            <div xml:id="d656">

                <pb facs="0793" n="785" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg785"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.635 Neep/63</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gordon</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 279</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">The
                                Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1938-04-13"
                            >April 13, 1938</date>.<lb/>[Received April 25.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Potential Liberian Iron Ore Concession</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my telegram No. 17 of
                    February 14, the Department’s answering telegraphic instruction No. 12 of
                    February 16, and my despatch No. 207 of March 7, in the above entitled matter, I
                    have the honor to report that a few days ago Mr. van Nierop called me on the
                    telephone and said that while he had not yet received the awaited report from
                    the engineers in Liberia, all developments seemed to be progressing favorably.
                    He stated that he had given full consideration to the matters discussed in our
                    recent conversation (see my despatch No. 207), and that he would be glad to have
                    the United States Steel Corporation “either as associates or customers” in this
                    projected iron ore exploitation; he felt, however, that the only logical way for
                    him to get in touch with the Corporation would be by approaching Morgan &amp;
                    Company, which he proposed to do, if and when he receives a favorable report
                    from Liberia.</p>
                <p>I am enclosing herewith a transcript of the pertinent parts of a letter<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d656-f1"/> written by Mr. van Nierop confirming the foregoing
                    telephone conversation.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">George A. Gordon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
